<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/foundational/society-socialisation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    You did not choose your language, manners, ambitions or sense of normal — you were
    <em>socialised</em> into them. Sociology studies how that happens and what it builds:
    <strong>norms</strong> (the unwritten rules — queue here, don't sing on the bus),
    <strong>roles</strong> (student, sibling, employee, each with a script),
    <strong>institutions</strong> (family, school, religion, media, the state) that reproduce
    them, and <strong>groups</strong>, whose pull turns out to be measurable in a laboratory
    with a line of the wrong length. The discipline's founding wager, made by Auguste Comte
    when he coined the name in 1838, is that society can be studied like nature — with
    patterns, rates and causes — and Émile Durkheim's <em>Suicide</em> (1897) made the
    founding demonstration: even the most intimate decision anyone makes has social
    fingerprints all over it, because suicide <em>rates</em> are stable year to year and
    differ systematically between groups. Individuals come and go; the pattern persists.
    That is a <strong>social fact</strong>, and it is sociology's object.
  </p>
  <p>
    The nature–nurture question is best settled by the rare natural experiments. Children
    raised in extreme isolation — the tragic case of "Genie", discovered in Los Angeles in
    1970 after more than a decade of near-total isolation — never acquired normal language
    or social behaviour despite intensive help afterward, evidence that our capacities need
    a social window to unfold in. Nobody is socialised <em>by</em> nothing; the question is
    never whether society shapes us, but <em>which</em> society, <em>when</em>, and
    <em>how much</em>.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the founders and the famous experiments — what sociology learned about pulling people around" widget="timeline" config={socTimeline} />

  <h2>How we learn our identity</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Primary socialisation</strong> happens in the family and early childhood, and it
    installs the deepest defaults: language (the accent and dialect that will read you to
    strangers for life), attachment, gendered expectations (which toys, which chores, which
    emotions are "for you"), and the class-inflected habits Bourdieu would later call taste.
    <strong>Secondary socialisation</strong> takes over as institutions widen: school teaches
    the formal curriculum — and, running silently beneath it, the <strong>hidden
    curriculum</strong> of punctuality, obedience to authority, queueing for rewards:
    Bowles and Gintis argued the correspondence between school hierarchy and workplace
    hierarchy is the point, not a by-product. Peer groups dominate adolescence (the only
    stage where you outrank your parents), media and its algorithms now socialise at a scale
    no family can match, and religion or its secular equivalents carry values and community.
    <strong>Anticipatory socialisation</strong> is the rehearsal — teenagers practising
    adulthood are doing sociology in the mirror — and <strong>resocialisation</strong> is the
    forced rewrite: army boot camps, prisons, migration. Erving Goffman called institutions
    that strip and rebuild identity <em>total institutions</em>, and boot camp is the honest
    demonstration: identity can be reinstalled by design.
  </p>
  <p>
    The theorists give the mechanism a face. Charles Cooley's <strong>looking-glass
    self</strong> (1902): we build our self-image from how we imagine others see us — three
    steps, imagination to imagination, which is why a teacher's casual comment can redirect a
    child. George Herbert Mead added the developmental ladder: children learn to take the role
    of the other (first by imitation, then by playing one role, finally by grasping "the
    generalized other" — society's expectations as a whole), which is how the social "me"
    forms around the spontaneous "I". Goffman's <em>presentation of self</em> (1959) made it
    theatre: a <em>front stage</em> performance in interviews and classrooms, a <em>back
    stage</em> self at home, with impression management running continuously — a sociology
    of everyday life so precise you recognise yourself in it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the agents of socialisation — every arrow installs part of you; click each one" widget="diagram" config={agentsCfg} />

  <h2>How society controls us</h2>
  <p>
    Control runs on two channels. <strong>Informal control</strong> is the everyday machine:
    smiles and praise (positive sanctions), sneers and gossip (negative ones), and the
    deep-installed version — shame and conscience — which works because norms are
    <em>internalised</em>: by adulthood, most policing is self-policing. You stop at an empty
    red light at 3 a.m.; the audience in your head is watching. <strong>Formal control</strong>
    is the state channel: laws, police, courts, graded punishments — needed because
    informal control fails at scale. The theoretical split comes over what socialisation is
    <em>for</em>. Functionalists (following Durkheim) see it as society's adhesive: shared
    norms produce solidarity, and even deviance is useful — it clarifies where the line is,
    and the scandal that follows a crime is the boundary being publicly re-drawn. Marxists
    see adhesive with a direction: the norms transmitted reproduce the existing class order
    and call it meritocracy. Both agree on the fact; they argue about the invoice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Deviance itself got a theoretical upgrade worth knowing. Robert Merton's <strong>strain
    theory</strong> starts from a gap: society sells everyone the same goals (success,
    wealth) but distributes the legitimate means unevenly — so some innovate (crime), some
    ritualise, some rebel against the goals themselves. And <strong>labelling theory</strong>
    (Howard Becker) relocates part of deviance in the reaction: call a kid a troublemaker
    often enough and the label becomes a master status, the "selves" others reflect back
    harden accordingly — a self-fulfilling prophecy demonstrated experimentally in Rosenthal
    and Jacobson's 1968 "Pygmalion in the Classroom", where teachers told randomly selected
    pupils were "bloomers" produced measurable IQ gains in those pupils by year's end. The
    label helped cause the behaviour. Control, socialisation and identity turn out to be the
    same loop seen from three angles.
  </p>

  <h2>What shapes identity: class, gender, ethnicity — together</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus's three master-influences are lenses, not boxes. <strong>Social class</strong>
    works through Bourdieu's <em>habitus</em>: the tastes, speech codes and bodily manner
    absorbed at home (cultural capital) that schools quietly reward and employers quietly
    read — sociolinguist Basil Bernstein's "restricted" and "elaborated" language codes made
    the mechanism concrete. <strong>Gender</strong> is socialised from the first pink or blue
    item: Ann Oakley documented how toys, chores and praise split by sex long before children
    can articulate it, and the modern formulation — "doing gender" — treats it as a
    performance renewed daily rather than a possession. <strong>Ethnicity</strong> shapes
    identity through community, heritage and, unavoidably, others' reactions — from pride and
    dual heritage to the labour of code-switching. And since everyone is all of these at
    once, Kimberlé Crenshaw's <strong>intersectionality</strong> (1989) supplies the summing
    rule: a working-class teenage girl from a migrant family experiences neither class nor
    gender nor ethnicity separately but as one compounded position, which is why single-axis
    explanations keep missing people. Identity, finally, is <em>negotiated</em>, not
    installed: people resist, remix and renegotiate every script — which is precisely why
    socialisation must keep running all our lives.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> socialisation is not brainwashing, and the self is not
  society's puppet. The evidence for enormous social influence — Asch, Milgram, advertising,
  every classroom — sits alongside the evidence for resistance: subcultures built against
  their parents' norms, individuals who dissent against unanimous groups the moment one ally
  does, identities invented wholesale by people their scripts never anticipated. Nurture
  loads the gun; agency still aims it. Both halves of that sentence are the finding.</p>

  <h2>Roles, and what happens when they collide</h2>
  <p>
    The role concept deserves its own section because its complications are where real
    lives get lived. Holding many roles at once — student, employee, teammate, daughter or
    son, carer of a grandparent — works smoothly until the scripts conflict: the exam
    revision that clashes with the shift you promised to cover, the friendship that
    contradicts the job. Sociologists call the clash between <em>different</em> roles
    <strong>role conflict</strong>, and the strain within <em>one</em> over-stuffed role
    <strong>role strain</strong> — a student role that demands study, wellbeing, sociability
    and self-marketing simultaneously is the modern example. People manage these the way
    Goffman predicted: by scheduling, by quarantining audiences ("my work friends never meet
    my school friends"), by prioritising, and by quietly renegotiating the scripts. The
    <strong>life course</strong> adds the time dimension: roles arrive and retire on a
    schedule society sets (school-leaving, working age, retirement), and who gets to move
    through that sequence smoothly — and who is derailed by caring duties, unemployment or
    ill health — is itself sharply patterned by class and gender, which is how the micro
    world of roles connects back to the macro structure sociology exists to describe.
  </p>

  <h2>Studying society: how sociologists actually know</h2>
  <p>
    Sociology's claims are only as good as its methods, and the toolkit is worth knowing
    before trusting anyone's — including this page's. <strong>Primary data</strong> is
    collected fresh: <em>questionnaires</em> reach large numbers cheaply but flatten answers
    into boxes; <em>interviews</em> (structured to unstructured) get depth at the cost of
    scale; <em>participant observation</em> — the researcher joining the group, as
    sociologists have joined gangs, factories and football crowds — yields unmatched
    understanding that is impossible to generalise from honestly. <strong>Secondary
    data</strong> reuses existing material: official statistics (births, crime, exam
    results — free, large, and collected for someone else's purposes, which is exactly the
    caveat), historical documents, and other researchers' studies. Sampling decides
    everything in between: a <em>representative</em> sample lets a thousand people speak for
    a million; a convenience sample (whoever's nearby) makes a thousand people speak only
    for themselves. And every project runs the <strong>PET</strong> gauntlet — Practical
    (time, money, access), Ethical (informed consent, confidentiality, harm — the lesson
    Milgram taught the whole field), Theoretical (does the method actually capture what it
    claims?). When you read "studies show", these questions are the sieve.
  </p>
  <p>
    The methods pair with the theory in a way that explains half the arguments in the
    subject. Positivists, following Durkheim, favour large-scale quantitative data because
    they want sociology to find laws like physics does; interpretivists favour qualitative
    depth because they doubt society has laws at all — only meanings, which must be
    understood from inside (the German distinction is <em>Erklären</em>, explaining, versus
    <em>Verstehen</em>, understanding). Ask "is crime rising?" and the positivist reaches
    for recorded-crime series; the interpretivist points out that "crime" is whatever gets
    reported, recorded and defined, so the same society can produce two opposite series from
    one reality. Both are right, which is why the strongest studies triangulate — using
    each method's strength to cover the other's blind spot.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Durkheim's <em>Suicide</em> repays a second look because it is the discipline's
    methodological charter. He had no interest in individual motives; he aggregated
    European death records and found rates stable for years and patterned by religion
    (Protestants higher than Catholics — he argued less tightly integrated), by marriage,
    by crisis. His types — egoistic (too little integration), anomic (norms collapsed, as
    in sudden upheaval) — turned a private tragedy into an index of social health, and the
    move still powers every study that finds social patterns in individual outcomes.
    The experiments have their own afterlife: Milgram's obedience findings replicated
    broadly (Burger's 2009 rerun got almost identical rates with tighter ethics), but the
    deception-and-distress backlash they triggered built the entire modern research-ethics
    apparatus — a case where sociology's methods were disciplined by sociology's own
    findings. The newest chapter is algorithmic: feeds now select which versions of
    everyone we see, recommendation engines are socialisers that update a million times a
    day, and whether "looking-glass self" still describes a mirror or a hall of curated
    mirrors is the question this subfield is currently writing.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Durkheim, <em>Suicide</em> (1897); Cooley (1902); Mead, <em>Mind, Self and Society</em> (1934); Asch (1951); Milgram (1963); Goffman, <em>The Presentation of Self in Everyday Life</em> (1959); Tajfel & Turner (1979); Bourdieu, <em>Distinction</em> (1984); Rosenthal & Jacobson (1968); Burger (2009).</p>
